Using LLM to Generate Data for D3.js Force Directed Graph (FDG)
Jun Jun M. Zaragosa
·
Follow
5 min read
·
Jan 14, 2025
--
Listen
Share
FGD relating distinct set of ideas. Left: Compounds in mushroom. Right: Relation of iron absorption, coffee, pregnancy, bitter gourd, and caffeine.
D3.js
force-directed graph
is a type of visualization created using
D3.js
JavaScript library. This type of graph is commonly used to represent relationships between objects in network, such as social networks, communication network or dependencies. To render a force directed graph, we need to define the following:
Nodes
represents entities (e.g., people, objects, or concepts) and are displayed often as circles.
Links
represents the relationship between nodes.
Refer to
Interactive & Dynamic Force-Directed Graphs with D3.js
for detailed explanation on how nodes and links data are formatted and rendered using the D3.js.
In this post, I’ve used Node.js to demonstrate how to use LLM to generate data for D3.js force-directed graph. For the LLM, I used Google’s Gemini model to generate the nodes and links data based on prompt. Obtain your API key from
Google AI Studio.
Install the
GoogleGenerativeAI
package using the following npm command:
npm install @google/generative-ai
To work with the D3.js library in the front end, the easiest way is to use the D3.js’s CDN. Place the following inside the head of the web page or inside the body tag.
<script src="https://d3js.org/d3.v6.min.js"></script>
The following JavaScript function renders a force-directed graph in the web page. The
svgSelector
is a DOM element reference to the svg tag we want to render the force-directed graph.
function load_force_directed_graph({ nodes, links, svgSelector, width, height })
{
width = width || window.innerWidth * 0.6;
height = height || window.innerHeight * 0.8;
// Select the SVG element and set dimensions
const svg = d3.select(svgSelector)
.attr("width", width)
.attr("height", height);
//remove existing FDG
svg.selectAll("*").remove();
// Create a group element to wrap the graph content
const graphGroup = svg.append("g");
// Create a simulation for the force-directed graph
const simulation = d3.forceSimulation(nodes)
.force("link", d3.forceLink(links).id(d => d.id).distance(150))
.force("charge", d3.forceManyBody().strength(-200))
.force("center", d3.forceCenter(width / 2, height / 2));
// Create the links (edges) between nodes
const link = graphGroup.append("g")
.selectAll(".link")
.data(links)
.enter().append("line")
.attr("class", "link")
.attr("stroke-width", 2)
.attr("stroke", "#aaa");
// Create the nodes
const node = graphGroup.append("g")
.selectAll(".node")
.data(nodes)
.enter().append("circle")
.attr("class", "node")
.attr("r", 10)
.attr("fill", "steelblue")
.call(d3.drag()
.on("start", dragStart)
.on("drag", dragMove)
.on("end", dragEnd));
// Add labels to the nodes
const labels = graphGroup.append("g")
.selectAll(".label")
.data(nodes)
.enter().append("text")
.attr("class", "label")
.attr("dy", -15)
.text(d => d.label);
// Create the tooltip element
const tooltip = d3.select(".tooltip");
// Update positions on each tick of the simulation
simulation.on("tick", () =>
{
link
.attr("x1", d => d.source.x)
.attr("y1", d => d.source.y)
.attr("x2", d => d.target.x)
.attr("y2", d => d.target.y);
node
.attr("cx", d => d.x)
.attr("cy", d => d.y);
labels
.attr("x", d => d.x)
.attr("y", d => d.y);
});
// Dragging functions
function dragStart(event, d)
{
if (!event.active) simulation.alphaTarget(0.3).restart();
d.fx = d.x;
d.fy = d.y;
}
function dragMove(event, d)
{
d.fx = event.x;
d.fy = event.y;
}
function dragEnd(event, d)
{
if (!event.active) simulation.alphaTarget(0);
d.fx = null;
d.fy = null;
}
// Mouseover and mouseout events for the tooltip
node.on("mouseover", function (event, d)
{
tooltip.transition().duration(200).style("opacity", 0.9);
tooltip.html(d.info)
.style("left", (event.pageX + 5) + "px")
.style("top", (event.pageY - 28) + "px");
highlightAdjacentNodes(d);
})
.on("mouseout", function ()
{
tooltip.transition().duration(200).style("opacity", 0);
resetHighlights();
});
// Add zoom functionality
svg.call(d3.zoom().on("zoom", (event) =>
{
graphGroup.attr("transform", event.transform);
}));
}
Generating Nodes and Links from Prompt
In this section, i’ve demonstrated how to generate the data for nodes and links using the Gemini model.
From the front-end of the web application, we are using Axios to access the backend API, which will make a request to the Google Gemini model server. We are sending a POST request, since it involves sending data from the front-end to the backend. The first parameter is the route that will handle the request. The second parameter is an object containing the data we want to send to the backend API.
